.TH QUANTUM_BALL 1 "October 2026" "quantum_ball 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
quantum_ball \- classical and quantum ball bouncing in a box under gravity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quantum_ball
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR classical | quantum | eigen ]
.RB [ \-\-state
.IR N,M ]
.RB [ \-\-frames
.IR COUNT ]
.RB [ \-\-record
.IR DIR ]
.br
.B quantum_ball
.BR \-\-selftest " | " \-\-version " | " \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B quantum_ball
is an interactive SDL2 demonstration of the same problem solved classically
and quantum mechanically: a particle in a rectangular box with hard walls
under constant gravity. Units are normalised (hbar = m = 1); gravity points
down the screen, so the potential is V = \-g y and the floor is the bottom
wall.
.TP
.B Classical mode
A point mass with elastic wall bounces. The panel shows its energy and the
drift since the last reset.
.TP
.B Quantum mode
A Gaussian wave packet launched with the same angle and speed, evolved with
the time-dependent Schrodinger equation by Strang split-step propagation in
a sine (DST-I) pseudospectral basis, which builds the hard walls into the
basis. The method is unitary; the panel shows the norm, the energy
expectation and its drift. The white dot marks <x>,<y>.
.TP
.B Eigenstate mode
The stationary states psi_{N,M}(x,y) = sin(N pi x / Lx) phi_M(y). The
vertical part is computed by Numerov shooting. For strong gravity these are
the Airy states of the quantum bouncer: the panel shows the exact energy and
the Airy approximation E = Ex \- g Ly + |a_M| (g^2/2)^(1/3), where a_M is the
M-th zero of Ai. The arrow keys change N (left/right) and M (up/down).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " M"
Start in the given mode (default classical).
.TP
.BI \-\-state " N,M"
Start in eigenstate mode showing psi_{N,M}.
.TP
.BI \-\-frames " COUNT"
Run that many frames and exit. With
.B \-\-record
the frame time is fixed at 1/60 s so the output is deterministic.
.TP
.BI \-\-record " DIR"
Write every frame to DIR/frame_NNNNN.ppm. Works without a display:
.B SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy quantum_ball \-\-record ...
.TP
.B \-\-selftest
Without opening a window, propagate a packet for 3000 steps and check that
the norm and energy are conserved, and compute three eigenstates and check
that they are stationary. Exit status 0 on success.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-h
Print the version or usage and exit.
.SH CONTROLS
.TP
.B Mouse
Sliders set launch angle, speed, gravity and box size (any change resets).
Buttons switch modes, reset and pause.
.TP
.B Keys
.B C
classical,
.B Q
quantum,
.B E
eigenstates,
.B R
reset,
.B Space
pause,
.B Esc
quit. In eigenstate mode the
.B arrow keys
select the quantum numbers.
.SH NUMERICS
The grid has 20 points per unit length (clamped to 120\-400 by 80\-320) and
the time step is 0.0022 with three steps per frame. The default packet has
momentum 28, i.e. about 4.5 grid points per wavelength. Higher speeds need a
finer grid; the display will show it when the wavelength is under-resolved.
.PP
The split-step method conserves the norm exactly and the energy to O(dt^2);
in double precision the self-test reports a norm drift of about 1e-13 and an
energy drift of about 1e-7 over 3000 steps.
.SH SEE ALSO
Nesvizhevsky et al., "Quantum states of neutrons in the Earth's
gravitational field", Nature 415, 297 (2002), for the real quantum bouncer.
.SH AUTHOR
Mico Mrkaic. GPL-3.0-or-later.
